BSD Bob the old greybead BSD freak <bobkeys@weedcon1.cropsci.ncsu.edu> writes:
> If I do want to downconvert, what boards will be needed.  As I see
> it, the TK50 and ram cards are fine, from the existing set.  I would
> need a KA630 or KA650 cpu, a DELQA ethernet, and a QD24 Emulex ESDI
> controller and/or a CQD-223 SCSI controller.  What other cards
> would work, or should I look out for?

  Be careful! The MVII/KA630 does *not* use the same memory boards as the
later systems (it's limited to 16MB). The Q-bus doesn't have the address
bits necessary to address large memories, so the MicroVAXen (after the
MV I) use a "skybus" on a ribbon cable to get the extra bits to the board
(and eliminate contention on the Q-bus). There are a bunch of signal in-
tegrity problems on the KA630 memory bus, which requires a shielded cable
between the boards (which was an ECO). The pin that the KA630 used for the
ground on that cable is *not* a ground on the KA650 and later memories, so
you have to use an *unshielded* cable on those.

  All of the KA655 memories work on the KA650, but the reverse isn't true -
some of the old boards are too slow for the KA655.

  Note that putting a KA630 in a BA2xx is an unsupported config (it's never
been tested/qualified by DEC) so there's no official cabinet kit for it -
you'll have to do disgusting things with the cabkit. This is because nobody
ever bought a new box to put their old CPU in, though the reverse is true
(and, in fact, there are cabkits for all MicroVAXen up to the 4000-200 to
go into BA23/BA123 boxes). The 4000-300 and higher models no longer used
the Q-bus in the processor compartment, though they still had Q-bus slots
for peripheral controllers.

