John E. Malmberg <malmberg@eisner.decus.org> writes:
> Since the DSSI devices look like MSCP devices, shoudn't the
> same driver that is used on RDQX and KDA controllers for
> NetBSD basically work?
>
> From what I am seeing on VMS they all use DUDRIVER.
>
> If it does work, it means that if NetBSD can use the
> imbedded ethernet controller for the MicroVAX 3400,
> he should be in business.

  Nope. The physical programming for DSSI is different from the Unibus/
Q-bus controllers which are different from the MSCP server protocol. The
VMS driver for DSSI disks masks this and makes them all DUA disks, while
there are 2 (or 4, on the A-series 4000 models) DSSI buses and the disks
are natively DIAx/DIBx (and DICx/DIDx on the A-series).

  Traditionally, 4BSD-based Unixes bundled the port and class driver to-
gether instead of using a PK and a Dx device like VMS, but I think this
is one of the things that's changed in the Quasi-whatsit release that the
original poster wants to run. I doubt it supports the SHAC, though.

>>   You're correct that he could get a KA630 CPU and a
>>   KA65x S-box cabkit.
>
> And cut the CPU speed of the box in half.

  He's the one that suggested it - I want him to get the Ethernet on the
3300 working.

> They looked like they had the standard markings, but I do not
> really know.  Of course would the CSS custom build a slower
> version of a standard system for less money?

  No, for more money 8-).

