Interestingly, the NCR acquisition wasn't the first attempt of
AT&T's to get into the computer business...

...Just the most expensive... and rumor says NCR wasn't the first
choice.

Since this is over 15 years old and I'm long past any nondisclosure
arrangement...

I've never been able to confirm the below in any print media.

In about 83-84 (March 1984?) there was a near deal between DEC and AT&T and an
outsource of the AT&T data center Unix and other sysadmins 
and operators to DEC.

(my wife remembers talks in the AT&T Bell Labs news about stuff
like this in '84-85 timetable)

At about the same time AT&T came close to a merger/aquisition of DEC
which would've led them to sell and support SysV Unix on DEC Vax systems
and this would've allowed Unix to mature earlier and move into corporate
data systems a lot earlier than the late '80's.

It also would've given AT&T a successful computer product line which
would've avoided the disasterous failure of the 3B20 systems in the 
commercial and scientific market.

DEC would've gained some of the AT&T Bell Labs research and
microelectronics capability (and possibly avoided the money losing
Hudson fab plant). 

Imagine DEC moving earlier and quicker into microprocessors and
AT&T getting folks with COMMERCIAL and EDUCATIONAL computer market
experience to put Unix in the commercial market along with VAX/VMS
and the PDP11 OS's.

The deal supposedly fell through when AT&T told Ken Olson that they were
going to pick the new DEC management and VP's and Olson, supposedly,
told 'em where to stick it since DEC had made money in computers and 
modules since the PDP1 and the company's start in 1957 while. AT&T hadn't.

Anyone have the scoop from the AT&T side?  I think this history
needs to be documented.  The combined company would've been an
interesting competitor to everyone including IBM.

I was at DEC when the '84 deal was close and was approached about an
AT&T operations job as a DEC Field Service employee.

I later was at Pyramid when the NCR deal happened... AT&T used Pyramid
as their high-end System 7000 Unix box and damn near went into a coma as
AT&T cut their lifeline (AT&T was 50% of Pyramid's business... Siemens-Nixdorf
was another chunk, direct sales was the remainder.  Siemens finally
bought out Pyramid and ended up dumping it on Amdahl/Fujitsu recently).

I also worked at Concurrent Computer (nee Interdata, Perkin Elmer) and
rumors there included the chance at a major link up of the 8/32 and 7/32
with Unix which was killed because they weren't interested in model
changes to make the support of Unix better. 


