   wkb@freebie.demon.nl (Wilko Bulte) writes:

>>drives.  My guess is that since people would be using drives not
>>tested and blessed by Compaq, they don't want to get involved with
>>that.  :(
> 
> Once upon a time we had so called 'white box SBBs'. Empty ones, including
> flex etc for home-grown assembly. 
> 
> Trust me, you don't want to be in support with that kind of product out
> there. It was quickly removed from the pricelist... ;-)
> 

   Why not, support is easy:

    Do you have any white SBBs in the system?
    If yes, remove them and see if the problem goes away.

 This is pretty much the same approach that DEC always used for
support of 3rd party gear. Digital made it quite clear when
you bought one of these kits that they took no responsibility
for the performance of the drive you installed in it.

 Seems to me it's much more likely Digital removed these items
because they were cutting into the sales of preassebled bricks
and the substantial profit margin built into those items.

 I'll grant you it wasn't always easy to get the drives installed
properly - usually because of deficient documentation from the
drive manufacturer - the polarity on the SCSI ID leads was
usually the problem.

 There's an easy solution to that today - sell empty canisters
for SCA drives - they only have one connector, you'd have to
be a complete imbecile to assemble one of those wrong, and
if you do it's your problem, not Compaqs.





