> > Hello, all.
> > I'm a Ph.D student in Control area.  Anyone knows about
> > the job-marketing for control? In my university, don't know
> > why, it's like crazy. These two years, Computer Science has
> > become more and more hot, lots of graduate students from
> > Mechanical Engineering or even Electrical Engineering are
> > taking courses of java or database.Apparently, we can either
> > find job in control or java, but taking java or database course
> > won't help in control area job-hunting. Right?
> > Maggie
> >
> >

You should initiate your job acquisition strategy now and you have two 
paths (besides the trivial, jobless one): academia and industry. If you 
choose an academic career you had best be published by the time you 
start applying to programs, which should be in the last semester of your 
dissertation. When I say published you may discard those conferences 
your advisor may have sent you to or that article you might have written 
for "Sensors" magazine. We are talking refereed journals, the best being 
IEEE Trans. on Automatic Control although the "lesser" journals are 
fine. Right now a large number of the older guys are passing into 
retirement and so the academic openings are good.

As for industry, you you start reading some of the trade journals. IC&S 
and Sensors to name two. With some creative bingo card playing you can 
get subscriptions free. These will give you an idea of where the 
industry is. As to your original question, I will tell you that the 
engineer who is also a good programmer and somewhat savvy on the latest 
langauges is always ahead. Good programming skills will not make up for 
a weak preparation in controls, but it will enhance your marketability.

My final comment (yes, the bell is about to ring) is more philosophical. 
Try to resist following the masses just for financial gain or security. 
Do what your heart and mind want to do, not your pocketbook.

