Sorry, I should have been more specific.  I need the NT (ARC) ECU.  I did
finally locate a part number on COMPAQ/DIGITAL's web site but I was unable
to find out who to order the part from.  The part number I have is :

Kit Number (QA-01YAA-HC) which contains
V1.9 (AK-Q2CRJ-CA) for OpenVMS and Digital UNIX and
V1.9 (AK-PYCJK-CA) for Windows NT

Presumably, I need AK-PYCJK-CA.  Here is the interesting thing though.
Apparently, Compaq reps do not have any idea what these part numbers
represent nor how I would go about ordering them.  I did eventually find a
link on a linux-alpha forum to this address:
ftp://ftp.digital.com/pub/linux/alphalinux/Linux-Alpha/JENSEN_INSTALL/
which contained the file
jensen_ecu_disk.gz

After gunzipping the file, I used the linux rawrite utility to write the
image to a floppy.  This yeilded a usable EISA config disk that appeared to
work without error, however, I was still unsuccessful in my attempt to
install NT.

Once the EISA bus was (apparently) configured using this disk (which may or
may not be the right thing), I encountered the error:
"Could not load Ntkrnlmp.exe.  The Error Code is 4."

I looked up this error on Microsoft's Knowledgebase and found article number
Q175932.  According to the article, there are three possible causes for the
error.
1) The computer is running outdated firmware.
I do not think this is the problem since I updated the firmware to version
4.31 using the files on the digital ftp site.
2) Outdated Arcinst.exe was used to create the system partition
This may have something to do with my problem though the article said that
the NT 4.0 version of ArcInstall (which I am using from the CD-ROM) should
work.
3) The computer was configured using an EISA configuration utility (ECU) for
another operating system
This is the most likely problem given that my ECU was obtained in a Linux
directory.

In any case, I would appreciate any pointers to either a web or anon FTP
site or a helpful number at COMPAQ where I can order this.
Thanks in advance,
Robert Todd Edgel



