In article <8q5gh2$l0k$1@uni00nw.unity.ncsu.edu>, BSD Bob the old greybead BSD freak <bobkeys@weedcon1.cropsci.ncsu.edu> writes:
:I have had the good/mis/maybe-fun fortune to pick up a MicroVAX 3300...
:What kinds of drives and what sizes of drives can be added into a 
:MicroVAX 3300, internally or externally?

  The RF-series disks and TF-series tapes, obviously, can connect 
  directly to the DSSI bus.  Common RF-series disks (and respective
  capacities) include:

    RF30:  150  MB
    RF31:  381  MB
    RF35:  852  MB
    RF36:  1.6  GB
    RF71:  400  MB
    RF72:  1    GB
    RF73:  2.0  GB
    RF74:  3.57 GB

  Various SCSI disks can be connected via an HSD-series DSSI-SCSI 
  storage adapter (HSD05, HSD10, etc).

  There are third-party Q-bus to SCSI widgets around.

  The DIGITAL KZQSA -- a Q-bus to SCSI widget -- is not supported 
  for use with magnetic disks on various operating system platforms.
  (Some platforms might, but I know OpenVMS does not.)

:The CPU is a KA640.  Is there a better/other/scsi-compatible CPU
:board that I might put in, instead?

  There are certainly faster CPUs, but Q-bus systems don't generally
  hook particularly nicely or easily to SCSI.

  KA650, KA65, and KA660 will all work in this BA215 box.  The KA670 
  and upwards will require a different backplane.  (Please see the 
  OpenVMS FAQ for the CPU name to model name translation table.)

:This is a cute little machine, that might be fun to set up as a
:closet server of some sort, if I could wrangle some larger drives
:into it, or some scsi out of it.

  It's old and slow and rather limited.  In the BA215 enclosure
  common in the MicroVAX 3300, it's also particularly slot-limited.
  (The MicroVAX 3400 was the same CPU and memory, with more Q-bus 
  slots and with more room in the enclosure for storage widgets.)

  The Q-bus has a bandwidth limit of roughly 3.3 megabytes per second 
  using block mode transfers, and rather less bandwidth available with 
  other (and rather common) bus transfer modes.

:Lastly, anyone have a spare manual for a MicroVAX 3300?  It would
:be nice to know a little bit more of the details of the machine.

  Compaq Assisted Services (CAS) is listed in the OpenVMS FAQ, and
  can be a useful source for diagnostics and documentation.

  I will assume that you know about the OpenVMS hobbyist program.  
  (If you are not, then please see the OpenVMS FAQ for details.)

  Details on accessing and configuring DSSI widgets are included
  in the OpenVMS FAQ.

 --------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
   Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman#xdelta.zko.dec.com




