In article <8qbrv5$b5f$1@slb7.atl.mindspring.net>,
  genew@netcom.com (Gene Wagenbreth) wrote:
> I have a DEC 3000. Recently, I have been having a problem.
> Sometimes when I turn it on, nothing happens. The green light
> on the front doesnt come on, nothing. I turn it off and on
> a bunch of times, or wait a while, and it works fine. I thought
> it was just the switch, so I left the switch on and used the
> switch on the power strip. Seemed to work for a while, but
> this morning it happened with the power strip. Had to turn
> it off and on several times. Everything else on the power
> strip works fine.

> Has anyone else had this problem ? Is it common ?

Coincidentally, this has just happened on one of our surplus
VAXstations I gave away. My "customer" is similarly perplexed. Simple
possibilities are (a) power connector worked loose -- they sometimes
need to be pushed in hard, (b) faulty switch or dry joint thereon, but
you've probably eliminated those by using the power strip. More
seriously, your PSU has a lazy switch-mode oscillator which will
eventually refuse to work ever.

> Is the
> problem the power suppy ?

Almost certainly. It could just be an internal short/overload, but then
I'd expect it to come on momentarily.

>  I am going to try to have DEC fix
> it. Anyone have any experience calling DEC maintenance ? I do
> not have a maint contract. Any idea how much it will cost ?

An arm and a leg, I should think. A DECpaq Field Servant will simply
swap the PSU. If it's your own money, it'll be cheaper to get a new PSU
from one of the surplus dealers and replace it yourself. On all the
3000 models I know, it's just a matter of a few screws and connectors.
If it's a 3000/300LX, the hardest bit is getting the case open and
closed, which operations carry serious risks of slashing fingers on
sharp metal bits. The manual tells you how it's supposed to be done.

Chris


