>-1) With (2) HSG80s in a Multipath (i.e. OpenVMS supported) failover
>configuration: What is the maximum number of I/Os recommended per SCSI
>port off the back end of the HSG80?  I am thinking about using the new
>15K rpm disks that will be doing for the most part 1 KB data transfers.
>But I will be doing a lot of them .  How many 1 KB transfers can be
>supported on each of these SCSI buses before they become clogged?

Ken>If you figure that a 40 MB/Sec SCSI bus saturates at about 30 MB/Sec due
Ken>to the overhead associated with smaller I/O operations, then simply divide
Ken>the 30 MB/Sec by the I/O size. For 1 KB, that comes to 30,000 I/O requests
Ken>per second (which is greater than the I/O rate capability of a single
Ken>HSG80). Thus, for 1 KB transfers, I would say that you shouldn't worry.

>2) What is the rule of thumb for maximum number of members in a
>stripeset?  I have been using 6 as the maximum; One for each port off of
>the HSG80.  I know the maximum is 24 according to the documentation but
>what are the issues involved in going beyond 6 disks?

Ken>No rule of thumb, and the same logic as the above. With a 24 member set,
Ken>you would have 4 disks per bus. At 30 MB/Sec per bus, that comes to the same
Ken>30,000 1KB requests per second total, or 7,500 per second per disk. A wee
Ken>bit beyond the capability of a disk! Figuring a disk at 200 requests per
Ken>second (and that's pushing it), 200 x 1 KB x 4 disks per bus is 1.6 MB/Sec.
Ken>Hardly stressing things.

>3) What is the number of I/Os that the HSG80 can handle over all?  Is
>24K correct? How is this number affected when doing mirrored cache and
>multipath configuration?

Ken>This really depends on the transfer size, reads vs. writes, and cache
Ken>hits vs. disk accesses, single or dual port. I hate to waffle on this, but
Ken>it really varies. If you can get some specifics, I can probably give you
Ken>some data.

>If anyone has real answers, not guesses, please respond.
>
>Many thanks in advanced
