In article <8pnh72$ifq$1@nina.pacific.net.au>, CSABA  HARANGOZO   <csabah@zipworld.com.au> writes:
:JMan <jman@urlgray.com> wrote:
:> I need to upgrade my VS3100 from V6.1 to 7.2. 

  Please see the OpenVMS FAQ for the various upgrade paths -- specifically
  on OpenVMS VAX, there is slight wrinkle (a BACKUP ECO) needed if you want 
  to directly upgrade from OpenVMS VAX V6.1 to OpenVMS VAX V7.2.  The name 
  of the kit needed here is listed in the FAQ.)

:> ...
:> I used to work at DEC, even taught this stuff is the System Manager
:> courses, but cannot remember the details.

  You _do_ remember what the manuals looked like, eh?  :-)

:> 1. Procedure to create standalone boot capability on disk. Do I run
:> STABACKIT to create the standalone root?

  Yes.  I'd recommend creating it on a disk other than the system disk.

  But the standard OpenVMS VAX distribution itself has a standalone
  BACKUP root, and the recent OpenVMS VAX distributions have a bootable
  OpenVMS root -- I do not know what is on the hobbyist kit off-hand,
  but I would expect at least the standalone root exists.

:> 2. Boot command for standalone. >>>B r3:00000E is this the correct
:> command to boot from [SYSE]?
:
:	Well, from memory also :
:	>>>B/E0000000 DKx00:   ( or >>>B/R5:E0000000  DKx00: )

  Both B/E0000000 DKx00: and B/R5:E0000000 DKx00: are correct for
  most VAX consoles -- the /R5 syntax is slightly newer.
  
  On a VAX console, the boot root is in the upper nibble of R5.
  (See the FAQ for the VMB flags.)

  If the standalone root is built on another disk, it is typically
  built in root SYS0, meaning the upper nibble of R5 is zero,
  and thus R5 can and often is entirely zero.

:> Then I need to do the upgrade. This part is a bit more foggy
:
:> 1. Can I just use VMSINSTAL? Which save set should I use ".A" or ".B"?

  You should be able to VMSINSTAL to upgrade.  You do not specify the
  extension, just VMS072.

  If you were to boot standalone and BACKUP/IMAGE VMS062.B/SAVE dkcu:,
  you would install OpenVMS -- and clobber the entire contents of the
  target disk.  Then boot the target disk to complete the installation.

:> 2. I need to keep DECnet and UCX running on this machine. Any idea if
:> the upgrade will break these, or would they have to updated as well?

  I would.  New versions of DECnet and TCP/IP Services

:> need to retain DECnet connectivity with a much older VAX which is
:> running 4.x and is running DECNET IV.

  DECnet Phase IV is available.  Depending on the OpenVMS release,
  you will be prompted for Phase IV or DECnet-Plus, or for just
  DECnet-Plus.  DECnet-Plus is backward-compatible with DECnet
  Phase IV.  (It also gives you stuff such as DECnet over IP.)
  But if you want it, DECnet Phase IV is available and can be 
  installed.

:> 3. The system has license PAKs on it. Would any of these need to
:> changed or upgraded as well?

  It depends on the PAKs.  You can LICENSE ISSUE the PAKs you have to 
  extract and get full (text or DCL) copies you can save off to the side, 
  and you can then reload the PAKs (ISSUE removes them) or (more likely)
  just acquire and load and use the OpenVMS Hobbyist PAKs.

