The man pages for chpt (the partition utility) and newfs (the new file
system utility) explain
this quite well; see them for details. 
For bootblocks, note that newfs *without* -n option will install
bootblocks when you use it to make a file system.
So for a completely new disk at rz1, do the following

newfs -v /dev/rrz1a /dev/rrz1a # puts a file system on a partition,
using some reasonable defaults..
chpt -q /dev/rrz1a # lets you see what is on a
chpt -pa 0 A-SIZE /dev/rrz1c # replace A-SIZE by size you want a to be,
note use partition c to address it...
chpt -pb B-OFFSET B-SIZE /dev/rrz1a # etc for b partition, swap usually
..... etc for sizes
newfs -v /dev/rrz1a /dev/rrz1a
newfs -v -n /dev/rrz1d /dev/rrz1a # if you're making file system on
partion d
.... etc

