In article <8p25tk$otk$1@nnrp1.deja.com>, tonkmasta@my-deja.com wrote:

> Anyone know where I can get some information on these ethernet cards 
> that plug in to the rear of the printer, specifically I want to be 
> able to change the ip address. There doesn't seem to be a config 
> utility in the menu system with the printer to do this and I don't 
> seem to be able to telnet to the ip address that it is configured to 
> be (160.100.41.254) Maybe I need to use ARP ??

You can use ARP to change the address, but only if it has no address or 
is at factory defaults.

One way to reset the IP address is to return the network card to factory 
defaults.  I believe you turn off the printer, remove the card, set one 
of the jumpers to "on" (I forget which one but I could find the docs if 
you want) and return the card to the printer.  Power on the printer, 
wait a minute and then turn the printer off and remove the card.  Reset 
the jumper to "off" and when you put the card back in the printer and 
turn it on, the network card is at factory defaults.

There are other ways to reset the address, but the software is a bit 
hard to find.  If you have a Mac, I could send you the NPmanage program 
which easily changes the attributes, assuming AppleTalk is enabled on 
the card.  Otherwise, you're better off returning the card to factory 
defaults.

Paul

